1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,200
Fine Print: an iPhone app that checks contracts against Ontario law

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:08,000
Paste a contract, import a PDF, or photograph a paper one

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Let's try a sample lease

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,200
Before anything leaves the phone, it asks for consent

5
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:20,100
The AI only reads: it tags clauses and must quote each one word for word

6
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:27,000
Code checks every quote, then cited Ontario rules decide

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:37,000
8 clauses conflict with the law. Read in 2.8 seconds

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Each verdict names the exact section of the Residential Tenancies Act

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Common patterns are only "worth a look": no legal claim

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Every finding quotes the contract word for word

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Tap a clause to see it highlighted in the original document

12
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,600
with what the law says, and a link to the official statute

13
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:15,000
Now a photo of a paper lease

14
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
The text is read on the phone itself, with Apple's Vision OCR

15
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Same checks on the scan: 3 conflicts with the law

16
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Last: a contractor agreement

17
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Not a lease or a job, so no Ontario rule applies

18
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,000
So no legal verdicts. Only patterns worth reading

19
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,700
Try it yourself: link in the post
